                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_81\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit's diff modifies core/src/de/gg/util/TickCounter.java, removing the line `tickCount = maxTicks;` from the constructor (along with the associated javadoc note \"Has to be reset() first to get used.\"). This is the actual bug fix: in the original TickCounter constructor, `tickCount` was initialized to `maxTicks` at construction time. Since `update()` only runs its tick-processing loop `while (updateTime >= tickDuration && tickCount < maxTicks)`, and immediately after checks `if (tickCount == maxTicks)` to signal that the round/period is over, initializing `tickCount` to `maxTicks` in the constructor caused `update()` to report \"finished\" on essentially the very first call, before any real ticks had been processed \u2014 unless `reset()` was manually called first. This produced the bug that had to be fixed here (rounds ending instantly / requiring an easy-to-forget manual reset before first use).\n\nSearching all 82 candidates for references to TickCounter.java, only two candidates touch this file: candidate_81 and candidate_82. candidate_81's diff shows TickCounter.java being added as a brand-new file (`new file mode 100644`), and its constructor already contains the problematic line:\n\n    this.tickHandler = tickHandler;\n    this.maxTicks = maxTicks;\n    this.tickDuration = tickDuration;\n\n    tickCount = maxTicks;\n\nThis is exactly the faulty initialization that the fix commit later removes. Since candidate_81 is the commit that first introduces TickCounter.java (and thus first introduces this erroneous initialization), it is the bug-introducing commit. No earlier candidate contains this file or logic (confirmed via search), so the bug could not have been introduced before candidate_81.\n"
